1
00:00:01,010 --> 00:00:03,385
July 23, 2024.

2
00:00:03,425 --> 00:00:07,165
Biscuit Basin, two miles
from Old Faithful.

3
00:00:07,205 --> 00:00:10,373
A little before 10 in the
morning, people are walking

4
00:00:10,413 --> 00:00:13,500
the boardwalk past a hot
pool called Black Diamond.

5
00:00:15,290 --> 00:00:16,530
Then the ground lets go.

6
00:00:18,230 --> 00:00:23,540
A column of steam, mud and rock
blasts about 600 feet into the air.

7
00:00:23,580 --> 00:00:28,370
Boulders the size of armchairs land
on the boardwalk. Tourists run.

8
00:00:30,190 --> 00:00:34,135
Nobody was hurt. But here's the
part that should make you sit up.

9
00:00:34,175 --> 00:00:39,094
Yellowstone is one of the most
closely watched places on Earth, with

10
00:00:39,134 --> 00:00:43,470
seismometers, GPS stations and
satellites all pointed at it.

11
00:00:43,510 --> 00:00:45,780
And none of those
instruments saw it coming.

12
00:00:47,550 --> 00:00:50,824
Every year, millions of people
come here expecting the

13
00:00:50,864 --> 00:00:53,585
"supervolcano" they've
seen in documentaries.

14
00:00:53,625 --> 00:00:55,260
A countdown clock.

15
00:00:55,300 --> 00:00:57,140
An eruption that's "overdue."

16
00:00:58,940 --> 00:01:00,880
That story is mostly wrong.

17
00:01:00,920 --> 00:01:03,675
The truth about what's
under Yellowstone is

18
00:01:03,715 --> 00:01:06,510
stranger, and in some
ways more surprising.

19
00:01:08,330 --> 00:01:11,534
When we came here in
October of 2021, we filmed

20
00:01:11,574 --> 00:01:14,570
almost everything you'll
see in this series.

21
00:01:14,610 --> 00:01:18,714
And standing on those boardwalks,
with the ground hissing under our

22
00:01:18,754 --> 00:01:22,650
feet, we wanted to know one thing:
what is actually down there?

23
00:01:24,450 --> 00:01:25,690
This is Yellowstone.

24
00:01:35,910 --> 00:01:41,000
About 2.1 million years ago, the land
where Yellowstone sits tore open.

25
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:44,131
The Huckleberry Ridge eruption threw out

26
00:01:44,171 --> 00:01:47,810
roughly 2,400 cubic
kilometers of rock and ash.

27
00:01:47,850 --> 00:01:52,545
That's enough to bury the whole state of
Rhode Island nearly half a mile deep.

28
00:01:52,585 --> 00:01:57,673
Its ash has been found as far away
as sea-floor cores off California,

29
00:01:57,713 --> 00:02:02,470
part of a single ash layer traced
across about 3,000 kilometers.

30
00:02:04,150 --> 00:02:07,454
Scientists now think even
that may have been two

31
00:02:07,494 --> 00:02:10,520
separate eruptions,
about 6,000 years apart.

32
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:15,650
Either way, it's one of the largest
volcanic events known anywhere on Earth.

33
00:02:17,520 --> 00:02:23,240
The second came about 1.3 million
years ago, smaller but still enormous.

34
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:26,090
Its ash rock has a name you can visit.

35
00:02:26,130 --> 00:02:30,295
This is Mesa Falls, in Idaho,
just outside the park.

36
00:02:30,335 --> 00:02:36,250
The river here pours over layers left by
that very eruption, the Mesa Falls Tuff.

37
00:02:38,120 --> 00:02:40,645
The third is the one that
made the park we know.

38
00:02:40,685 --> 00:02:44,985
About 631,000 years ago, the
Lava Creek eruption emptied

39
00:02:45,025 --> 00:02:49,093
the magma beneath Yellowstone
so fast that the ground

40
00:02:49,132 --> 00:02:52,890
collapsed into a crater
about 85 kilometers long.

41
00:02:52,930 --> 00:02:55,350
Geologists call it a caldera.

42
00:02:55,390 --> 00:03:00,043
New research suggests it wasn't
one blast either, but a series of

43
00:03:00,083 --> 00:03:04,560
pulses, separated by gaps that
could have been days, or years.

44
00:03:06,260 --> 00:03:09,800
Much of what you drive through
today sits inside that crater.

45
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:14,320
It's so big that most visitors never
realize they're standing in it.

46
00:03:14,360 --> 00:03:19,446
The yellow walls of the Grand Canyon
of the Yellowstone are rock from later

47
00:03:19,486 --> 00:03:24,270
lava flows, softened and painted by
hot water over thousands of years.

48
00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:32,130
But here's a twist most people miss.

49
00:03:32,170 --> 00:03:33,940
The volcano didn't start here.

50
00:03:35,630 --> 00:03:40,240
About 16.5 million years ago, the
heat source under Yellowstone was

51
00:03:40,280 --> 00:03:44,335
beneath what's now northern
Nevada, near the Oregon border.

52
00:03:44,375 --> 00:03:46,155
The heat stayed put.

53
00:03:46,195 --> 00:03:49,976
The continent moved, sliding
southwest at about an inch

54
00:03:50,016 --> 00:03:53,420
a year, roughly as fast
as your fingernails grow.

55
00:03:55,220 --> 00:03:59,990
Again and again, as the land passed
over it, new calderas punched through.

56
00:04:00,030 --> 00:04:05,700
The result is a 500-mile trail of
old volcanoes across southern Idaho.

57
00:04:05,740 --> 00:04:10,370
You can drive it today: it's the
flat, wide Snake River Plain.

58
00:04:12,180 --> 00:04:15,873
When we drove in from Idaho
Falls, we were crossing that

59
00:04:15,913 --> 00:04:19,780
trail, one dead caldera after
another, without knowing it.

60
00:04:25,630 --> 00:04:27,730
So what's under Yellowstone right now?

61
00:04:27,770 --> 00:04:29,520
Not a giant lake of lava.

62
00:04:31,240 --> 00:04:36,280
In 2025, two studies in the journal
Nature gave the clearest picture yet.

63
00:04:38,030 --> 00:04:42,820
The first measured the electrical signals
of the rock at more than a hundred sites.

64
00:04:42,860 --> 00:04:45,790
It found that the magma bodies under the

65
00:04:45,830 --> 00:04:49,205
caldera are only about
9 to 15 percent melted.

66
00:04:49,245 --> 00:04:54,450
The rest is solid crystal, like a sponge
with a little liquid in its holes.

67
00:04:54,490 --> 00:04:57,170
That's far too little liquid to erupt.

68
00:04:59,050 --> 00:05:01,275
The second study did something wonderful.

69
00:05:01,315 --> 00:05:05,568
To see the top of the magma,
researchers drove a 53,000-pound

70
00:05:05,608 --> 00:05:09,510
truck across the park that
shakes the ground on purpose.

71
00:05:09,550 --> 00:05:13,365
More than 600 seismometers
listened to the echoes.

72
00:05:13,405 --> 00:05:17,042
They found a lid of magma
and gas bubbles about 3.8

73
00:05:17,082 --> 00:05:20,430
kilometers down, venting
slowly through cracks.

74
00:05:20,470 --> 00:05:23,800
One of the scientists called
it "steady breathing."

75
00:05:25,600 --> 00:05:27,080
And you can see that breathing.

76
00:05:27,120 --> 00:05:30,385
The ground of Yellowstone rises and falls.

77
00:05:30,425 --> 00:05:33,784
Near Norris, the land bulged about 12

78
00:05:33,824 --> 00:05:38,470
centimeters between 1996
and 2004, then sank again.

79
00:05:38,510 --> 00:05:42,575
In July 2025, it started rising once more.

80
00:05:42,615 --> 00:05:46,875
The USGS says that doesn't mean
an eruption is more likely.

81
00:05:46,915 --> 00:05:49,220
It's what a living volcano does.

82
00:05:54,860 --> 00:05:56,440
So, is Yellowstone overdue?

83
00:05:58,160 --> 00:06:03,347
The idea goes like this: the big
eruptions came about 700,000 years

84
00:06:03,387 --> 00:06:07,990
apart, and it's been more than
600,000 years, so we're due.

85
00:06:09,810 --> 00:06:14,920
The US Geological Survey says plainly:
"Yellowstone is not overdue."

86
00:06:14,960 --> 00:06:19,165
That average is built on just two
gaps between three eruptions.

87
00:06:19,205 --> 00:06:23,125
In the USGS's word, that
math is "meaningless."

88
00:06:23,165 --> 00:06:25,790
Volcanoes don't run on schedules.

89
00:06:27,440 --> 00:06:30,410
Their estimate of the odds of another

90
00:06:30,450 --> 00:06:34,885
super-eruption in any given
year is about 1 in 730,000.

91
00:06:34,925 --> 00:06:39,320
That's roughly the same as a
kilometer-wide asteroid hitting Earth.

92
00:06:39,360 --> 00:06:42,830
And USGS scientists say
they're "not convinced"

93
00:06:42,870 --> 00:06:46,230
there will ever be another
eruption that big.

94
00:06:48,060 --> 00:06:51,325
What about the stories of bison
and elk fleeing the park?

95
00:06:51,365 --> 00:06:54,485
Those go around the internet every year.

96
00:06:54,525 --> 00:06:59,462
In 2025, the USGS made the
rumors one of its top stories of

97
00:06:59,502 --> 00:07:03,720
the year, because, in their
words, none were true.

98
00:07:07,860 --> 00:07:11,941
But in the retelling, it got a little bit
more serious and suddenly there were these

99
00:07:11,981 --> 00:07:15,670
worries that animals were fleeing the
park ahead of some impending disaster.

100
00:07:15,710 --> 00:07:16,800
Of course, not true.

101
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:20,910
So if a super-eruption isn't
the real danger, what is?

102
00:07:26,580 --> 00:07:28,205
Go back to Biscuit Basin.

103
00:07:28,245 --> 00:07:30,520
That wasn't magma. It was water.

104
00:07:32,240 --> 00:07:34,081
This was not triggered
by volcanic activity.

105
00:07:34,121 --> 00:07:36,430
If it were, we would have
seen many different changes.

106
00:07:37,390 --> 00:07:41,630
Yellowstone's hot springs are fed
by water heated deep underground.

107
00:07:41,670 --> 00:07:47,175
Over time, minerals like silica can clog
the shallow pipes. Pressure builds.

108
00:07:47,215 --> 00:07:52,250
And when the rock finally cracks,
water flashes to steam all at once.

109
00:07:53,890 --> 00:07:57,053
The USGS says blasts the
size of Biscuit Basin

110
00:07:57,093 --> 00:08:00,395
happen roughly once every
decade or few decades.

111
00:08:00,435 --> 00:08:03,230
Tiny ones happen every year.

112
00:08:03,270 --> 00:08:06,360
And under the north end
of Yellowstone Lake sits

113
00:08:06,400 --> 00:08:09,295
Mary Bay, more than a
mile and a half across.

114
00:08:09,335 --> 00:08:11,534
It's the largest crater from a steam

115
00:08:11,574 --> 00:08:14,560
explosion ever documented
anywhere in the world.

116
00:08:16,430 --> 00:08:20,311
If the next big headline from
Yellowstone is an "eruption,"

117
00:08:20,351 --> 00:08:23,740
this is most likely what
it'll be: steam, not lava.

118
00:08:31,480 --> 00:08:33,450
The land here can also shake.

119
00:08:33,490 --> 00:08:39,365
On the night of August 17, 1959,
at 11:37 p.m., a magnitude 7.3

120
00:08:39,405 --> 00:08:44,850
earthquake struck just west of
the park, near Hebgen Lake.

121
00:08:46,660 --> 00:08:49,915
In Madison Canyon, a
mountainside gave way.

122
00:08:49,955 --> 00:08:55,545
Around 50 million cubic yards of rock slid
across the river and buried a campground.

123
00:08:55,585 --> 00:08:57,570
Twenty-eight people died.

124
00:08:57,610 --> 00:08:59,940
The slide dammed the Madison River and

125
00:08:59,980 --> 00:09:02,685
created a new lake that's
still there today.

126
00:09:02,725 --> 00:09:04,370
It's called Earthquake Lake.

127
00:09:06,210 --> 00:09:09,570
Inside the park, something
astonishing happened.

128
00:09:09,610 --> 00:09:15,230
By the next day, at least 289
springs had erupted as geysers.

129
00:09:15,270 --> 00:09:19,050
One hundred sixty of them had
never been seen erupting before.

130
00:09:20,840 --> 00:09:24,600
This is Steamboat Geyser, the
tallest active geyser in the world.

131
00:09:24,640 --> 00:09:27,985
Its big eruptions shoot
more than 300 feet.

132
00:09:28,025 --> 00:09:30,465
It had been mostly quiet for decades.

133
00:09:30,505 --> 00:09:32,990
It came back to life
after that earthquake.

134
00:09:33,030 --> 00:09:38,620
Then in 2018 it woke up again, and
erupted 48 times in 2019, a record.

135
00:09:38,660 --> 00:09:42,790
Last year, in 2025, it
erupted just three times.

136
00:09:42,830 --> 00:09:45,295
When we stood here, it was only steaming.

137
00:09:45,335 --> 00:09:48,410
The park calls it
"entirely unpredictable."

138
00:09:50,190 --> 00:09:52,150
Old Faithful is the opposite.

139
00:09:52,190 --> 00:09:58,025
It goes off about every hour and a
half, 100 to nearly 200 feet high.

140
00:09:58,065 --> 00:10:03,180
The 1870 Washburn expedition
named it for how reliable it was.

141
00:10:08,850 --> 00:10:12,555
This is Grand Prismatic Spring,
the largest hot spring in

142
00:10:12,595 --> 00:10:15,775
Yellowstone, wider than a
football field is long.

143
00:10:15,815 --> 00:10:17,855
Those colors aren't minerals.

144
00:10:17,895 --> 00:10:20,110
They're living mats of microbes.

145
00:10:20,150 --> 00:10:24,735
Each color is a different community,
sorted by how hot the water is.

146
00:10:24,775 --> 00:10:28,077
Pale blue at the center
where it's hottest, then

147
00:10:28,117 --> 00:10:31,180
yellow, orange and brown
as the water cools.

148
00:10:32,870 --> 00:10:36,295
In the 1960s, a scientist
named Thomas Brock came

149
00:10:36,335 --> 00:10:39,690
here to study what could
live in water that hot.

150
00:10:39,730 --> 00:10:44,369
In a small pool in the Lower Geyser
Basin, he and a student found

151
00:10:44,409 --> 00:10:48,545
a bacterium that thrives in
water hot enough to scald you.

152
00:10:48,585 --> 00:10:50,920
They named it Thermus aquaticus.

153
00:10:52,570 --> 00:10:56,444
Nearly twenty years later,
a chemist named Kary Mullis

154
00:10:56,484 --> 00:11:00,575
invented a way to copy DNA
millions of times, called PCR.

155
00:11:00,615 --> 00:11:04,285
It needed an enzyme that could
survive repeated heating.

156
00:11:04,325 --> 00:11:07,840
The enzyme came from Brock's
Yellowstone microbe.

157
00:11:07,880 --> 00:11:10,290
Mullis won the Nobel Prize.

158
00:11:10,330 --> 00:11:13,760
PCR changed medicine,
crime labs, and, much

159
00:11:13,800 --> 00:11:17,190
later, made modern COVID testing possible.

160
00:11:18,940 --> 00:11:24,775
In 1991, the rights to that
technology sold for $300 million.

161
00:11:24,815 --> 00:11:27,060
Yellowstone received nothing.

162
00:11:27,100 --> 00:11:30,082
That deal is part of why
parks now have rules for

163
00:11:30,122 --> 00:11:33,330
sharing the benefits of
research done on their land.

164
00:11:39,170 --> 00:11:41,900
One more survivor lives
on top of the volcano.

165
00:11:43,820 --> 00:11:48,180
By 1902, hunting had wiped out
the great herds of the West.

166
00:11:48,220 --> 00:11:52,390
In Yellowstone, only 23
wild bison were left.

167
00:11:52,430 --> 00:11:55,650
They were the last wild
bison in North America.

168
00:11:57,390 --> 00:12:03,099
The park bought 21 more from private
ranches, raised them at Mammoth and later

169
00:12:03,139 --> 00:12:08,185
at the Lamar Buffalo Ranch, and
slowly mixed them with the wild herd.

170
00:12:08,225 --> 00:12:11,405
Today, several thousand
bison roam Yellowstone.

171
00:12:11,445 --> 00:12:15,140
They've lived on this land
for more than 10,000 years.

172
00:12:20,780 --> 00:12:22,670
So that's what's under Yellowstone.

173
00:12:22,710 --> 00:12:26,277
Not a ticking bomb, but a
slow, breathing system that's

174
00:12:26,317 --> 00:12:29,400
been reshaping this land
for millions of years.

175
00:12:31,070 --> 00:12:36,165
But in its first years as a park, the
danger to Yellowstone wasn't the volcano.

176
00:12:36,205 --> 00:12:40,815
It was people. Poachers,
vandals, stagecoach robbers.

177
00:12:40,855 --> 00:12:43,680
And the government's answer
was to send in the Army.

178
00:12:46,040 --> 00:12:47,865
March 1894.

179
00:12:47,905 --> 00:12:51,465
Pelican Valley, deep in
Yellowstone's backcountry.

180
00:12:51,505 --> 00:12:55,645
The snow is deep, the wind is
hard, and an Army scout named

181
00:12:55,685 --> 00:12:59,510
Felix Burgess is following
a trail of snowshoe tracks.

182
00:13:01,120 --> 00:13:06,235
At the end of the trail, he finds a man
bent over a dead bison, knife in hand.

183
00:13:06,275 --> 00:13:08,670
His rifle is leaning out of reach.

184
00:13:10,290 --> 00:13:14,350
The man is Ed Howell, the most
notorious poacher in the park.

185
00:13:14,390 --> 00:13:18,810
He's been killing Yellowstone's
last wild bison for their heads.

186
00:13:18,850 --> 00:13:22,130
And when he sees the scout,
he throws up his hands.

187
00:13:23,750 --> 00:13:27,220
This is the real photograph,
taken after his capture.

188
00:13:27,260 --> 00:13:30,845
Howell had killed at least
11 bison that winter.

189
00:13:30,885 --> 00:13:34,590
At the time, there was almost
no law to punish him for it.

190
00:13:36,390 --> 00:13:38,840
Within two months, that would change.

191
00:13:38,880 --> 00:13:44,120
And the reason it changed is one of the
strangest chapters in the history of

192
00:13:44,160 --> 00:13:49,510
America's parks: for 32 years, Yellowstone
was run by the United States Army.

193
00:13:59,820 --> 00:14:01,540
It started with pictures.

194
00:14:01,580 --> 00:14:08,085
In 1871, a government survey led by
Ferdinand Hayden explored Yellowstone.

195
00:14:08,125 --> 00:14:13,840
With it came a photographer, William Henry
Jackson, and a painter, Thomas Moran.

196
00:14:15,670 --> 00:14:20,609
Jackson hauled a huge camera, glass
plates and a portable darkroom on mules,

197
00:14:20,649 --> 00:14:25,260
developing every image on the spot,
sometimes minutes after he took it.

198
00:14:25,300 --> 00:14:28,605
Moran sketched in watercolor,
then went home and

199
00:14:28,645 --> 00:14:31,880
painted the canyon on a
canvas seven feet tall.

200
00:14:31,920 --> 00:14:37,710
Congress later bought that painting for
$10,000, an enormous sum at the time.

201
00:14:39,560 --> 00:14:43,340
Most members of Congress had never
seen anything like Yellowstone.

202
00:14:43,380 --> 00:14:48,738
Jackson's photographs and Moran's
watercolor sketches showed them a place

203
00:14:48,778 --> 00:14:53,545
that looked unreal: boiling springs,
a golden canyon, waterfalls.

204
00:14:53,585 --> 00:14:59,638
On March 1, 1872, President Ulysses S.
Grant signed a law making Yellowstone

205
00:14:59,678 --> 00:15:04,890
the world's first national park,
as the idea is understood today.

206
00:15:06,670 --> 00:15:08,985
You may have heard how the idea was born.

207
00:15:09,025 --> 00:15:13,986
In 1870, explorers sat around a campfire
right here, at the meeting of two

208
00:15:14,026 --> 00:15:18,920
rivers, and one of them proposed that
the land should belong to everyone.

209
00:15:18,960 --> 00:15:20,650
It's a great story.

210
00:15:20,690 --> 00:15:22,555
It probably never happened.

211
00:15:22,595 --> 00:15:25,862
About 20 firsthand accounts
of that expedition

212
00:15:25,902 --> 00:15:28,810
survive, and not one of them mentions it.

213
00:15:28,850 --> 00:15:32,600
The story first appeared in
print more than 30 years later.

214
00:15:34,340 --> 00:15:36,345
What did happen is less romantic.

215
00:15:36,385 --> 00:15:40,035
Congress created the park, and
then gave it almost nothing.

216
00:15:40,075 --> 00:15:44,670
The first superintendent, Nathaniel
Langford, wasn't paid at all.

217
00:15:44,710 --> 00:15:47,645
In five years, he went
into the park twice.

218
00:15:47,685 --> 00:15:50,880
Once with the survey, and
once to throw out a squatter.

219
00:15:56,580 --> 00:15:58,600
So people did whatever they wanted.

220
00:15:58,640 --> 00:16:02,553
In one winter alone, hunters
killed some 3,000 elk

221
00:16:02,593 --> 00:16:06,110
in and around the park,
just for their hides.

222
00:16:06,150 --> 00:16:10,447
Tourists broke off pieces of geyser
cones as souvenirs, and threw

223
00:16:10,487 --> 00:16:14,290
logs and junk into geysers
just to see what would happen.

224
00:16:16,070 --> 00:16:20,941
In 1877, a new superintendent,
Philetus Norris, took over, and the

225
00:16:20,981 --> 00:16:25,405
next year Congress finally gave
him a salary and some money.

226
00:16:25,445 --> 00:16:30,590
He built a log blockhouse headquarters on
a hill above Mammoth, and he built roads.

227
00:16:30,630 --> 00:16:35,025
Parts of his route still form
the Grand Loop you drive today.

228
00:16:35,065 --> 00:16:38,811
He hired a tough mountain man
named Harry Yount as the park's

229
00:16:38,851 --> 00:16:42,140
first gamekeeper, often
called the first park ranger.

230
00:16:43,880 --> 00:16:47,015
But Norris had no real
power to arrest anyone.

231
00:16:47,055 --> 00:16:49,350
There was no law to enforce.

232
00:16:49,390 --> 00:16:52,560
Norris was pushed out in 1882.

233
00:16:52,600 --> 00:16:57,720
And in 1886, Congress stopped paying
anyone to run the park at all.

234
00:16:59,490 --> 00:17:02,690
With no one left to protect
the park, the Secretary

235
00:17:02,730 --> 00:17:05,485
of the Interior did the
only thing he could.

236
00:17:05,525 --> 00:17:07,350
He asked the Army for help.

237
00:17:13,170 --> 00:17:17,614
On August 17, 1886, Captain
Moses Harris rode into

238
00:17:17,654 --> 00:17:21,740
Mammoth with 50 men of
the First U.S. Cavalry.

239
00:17:21,780 --> 00:17:25,410
His new office was a
broken-down blacksmith shop.

240
00:17:25,450 --> 00:17:30,454
Army engineer Hiram Chittenden, who
wrote the park's first history, later

241
00:17:30,494 --> 00:17:35,400
called Harris "vigorous and uncompromising
in suppressing lawlessness."

242
00:17:37,130 --> 00:17:38,650
Harris didn't waste time.

243
00:17:38,690 --> 00:17:43,018
He threw out rule-breakers, stopped
livestock from grazing near the

244
00:17:43,058 --> 00:17:47,100
park's wonders, and posted soldiers
at the most famous sights.

245
00:17:48,760 --> 00:17:54,190
A couple of years later, 15 infantry
soldiers were stationed at Old Faithful.

246
00:17:54,230 --> 00:17:59,155
Besides guarding it, they started
explaining the geysers to visitors.

247
00:17:59,195 --> 00:18:04,495
Park historians say those talks were the
beginning of what rangers still do today.

248
00:18:04,535 --> 00:18:07,560
They became known as the Immortal Fifteen.

249
00:18:09,380 --> 00:18:13,330
Think about that the next time a
ranger explains a geyser to you.

250
00:18:13,370 --> 00:18:16,910
The first people to do it in
Yellowstone were soldiers.

251
00:18:18,530 --> 00:18:20,865
The Army was supposed to be temporary.

252
00:18:20,905 --> 00:18:23,425
It stayed for 32 years.

253
00:18:23,465 --> 00:18:28,425
Its camp at the foot of the terraces
became a real fort, Fort Yellowstone.

254
00:18:28,465 --> 00:18:32,350
By 1910, more than 300
soldiers lived here.

255
00:18:34,120 --> 00:18:36,706
Thirty-five of the fort's
buildings are still

256
00:18:36,746 --> 00:18:39,740
standing, and they're still
the park's headquarters.

257
00:18:39,780 --> 00:18:44,660
If you've ever driven through Mammoth,
you've driven through an old cavalry post.

258
00:18:46,350 --> 00:18:51,235
Soldiers lived in small stations across
the park, 16 of them by the end.

259
00:18:51,275 --> 00:18:53,800
In summer they patrolled on horseback.

260
00:18:53,840 --> 00:18:58,863
In winter, they patrolled on skis,
sometimes 20 miles a day, sleeping in

261
00:18:58,903 --> 00:19:03,785
small backcountry cabins and hunting
for poachers' tracks in the snow.

262
00:19:03,825 --> 00:19:06,505
Civilian Army scouts went with them.

263
00:19:06,545 --> 00:19:10,120
Some of their diaries still
survive in the park's archives.

264
00:19:11,770 --> 00:19:16,375
In 1896, a unit of Black
soldiers from the 25th Infantry,

265
00:19:16,415 --> 00:19:21,182
the famous Buffalo Soldiers,
even rode bicycles through the

266
00:19:21,222 --> 00:19:25,175
park, testing whether bikes
could replace horses.

267
00:19:25,215 --> 00:19:28,550
They posed for a photograph
on the terraces at Mammoth.

268
00:19:34,390 --> 00:19:38,570
Which brings us back to Ed Howell,
hands in the air in Pelican Valley.

269
00:19:40,380 --> 00:19:45,262
By luck, a writer named Emerson Hough
was in the park that winter, on an

270
00:19:45,302 --> 00:19:49,500
expedition with the official park
photographer, F. Jay Haynes.

271
00:19:49,540 --> 00:19:53,016
Hough sent word of the
capture to his editor, George

272
00:19:53,056 --> 00:19:56,370
Bird Grinnell, at the
magazine Forest and Stream.

273
00:19:58,200 --> 00:20:02,015
Grinnell was one of the most powerful
conservationists in the country.

274
00:20:02,055 --> 00:20:07,470
With his friend Theodore Roosevelt, he
had founded the Boone and Crockett Club.

275
00:20:07,510 --> 00:20:12,760
The magazine ran the story with
photographs, and the outrage spread fast.

276
00:20:14,410 --> 00:20:15,540
There's a twist here.

277
00:20:15,580 --> 00:20:19,943
According to Smithsonian librarians,
the gruesome photos of dead

278
00:20:19,983 --> 00:20:24,070
bison that ran with the story
weren't Howell's kills at all.

279
00:20:24,110 --> 00:20:26,740
They were older images from another hunt.

280
00:20:26,780 --> 00:20:28,200
But the point landed.

281
00:20:29,990 --> 00:20:36,120
On May 7, 1894, a new law was approved
to protect the animals of Yellowstone.

282
00:20:36,160 --> 00:20:37,775
Hunting was banned.

283
00:20:37,815 --> 00:20:42,320
Poachers could be fined up to
$1,000 and jailed for two years.

284
00:20:42,360 --> 00:20:46,455
Their guns, horses and
traps could be taken.

285
00:20:46,495 --> 00:20:48,862
The commander of the
park called it "the most

286
00:20:48,902 --> 00:20:51,470
fortunate thing that ever
happened to the park."

287
00:20:53,360 --> 00:20:56,220
The law came too late to
really punish Howell.

288
00:20:56,260 --> 00:20:58,020
But it was written because of him.

289
00:20:59,780 --> 00:21:02,875
Even with the new law, the
herd almost didn't make it.

290
00:21:02,915 --> 00:21:07,620
By 1902, only 23 wild bison
were left in the park.

291
00:21:13,460 --> 00:21:15,770
The soldiers had other problems too.

292
00:21:15,810 --> 00:21:21,855
By the early 1900s, tourists toured the
park in long lines of stagecoaches.

293
00:21:21,895 --> 00:21:26,140
And stagecoaches full of wealthy
travelers attracted robbers.

294
00:21:27,810 --> 00:21:33,668
On August 24, 1908, a few miles past
Kepler Cascades, on the road between Old

295
00:21:33,708 --> 00:21:39,030
Faithful and West Thumb, a single
masked man stepped out of the trees.

296
00:21:39,070 --> 00:21:43,105
One by one, he stopped the coaches
as they came around the bend.

297
00:21:43,145 --> 00:21:49,260
By the most-cited count, he robbed 17
of them, more than 170 passengers.

298
00:21:49,300 --> 00:21:50,830
He was never caught.

299
00:21:52,610 --> 00:21:57,580
Six years later, in 1914, it
happened again near Shoshone Point.

300
00:21:57,620 --> 00:22:00,505
This time, one passenger
took out his camera and

301
00:22:00,545 --> 00:22:03,735
photographed the robber in
the middle of the hold-up.

302
00:22:03,775 --> 00:22:08,485
Another coach quietly turned around
and raced back to raise the alarm.

303
00:22:08,525 --> 00:22:12,127
The passenger's picture,
along with witness testimony,

304
00:22:12,167 --> 00:22:15,296
helped convict a man named
Ed Trafton, although

305
00:22:15,336 --> 00:22:19,180
historians still debate whether
he was really the robber.

306
00:22:24,920 --> 00:22:28,123
In 1903, Theodore Roosevelt
laid the cornerstone

307
00:22:28,163 --> 00:22:30,960
of this arch at the park's north entrance.

308
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:35,750
Its inscription reads: "For the
benefit and enjoyment of the people."

309
00:22:35,790 --> 00:22:39,964
During that visit, the President
camped in the park, and soldiers

310
00:22:40,004 --> 00:22:43,570
from Fort Yellowstone guarded
his camp near Tower Fall.

311
00:22:45,310 --> 00:22:48,515
But the Army never wanted to
be a park service forever.

312
00:22:48,555 --> 00:22:52,965
In 1916, Congress created
the National Park Service.

313
00:22:53,005 --> 00:22:56,915
Some of the soldiers who loved
Yellowstone took off their

314
00:22:56,955 --> 00:23:00,350
uniforms and stayed on as
the first park rangers.

315
00:23:02,010 --> 00:23:03,405
It didn't go smoothly.

316
00:23:03,445 --> 00:23:06,365
The troops came back in 1917.

317
00:23:06,405 --> 00:23:10,234
Then, on October 31,
1918, the last soldiers

318
00:23:10,274 --> 00:23:13,880
marched out of Fort Yellowstone for good.

319
00:23:15,710 --> 00:23:17,165
They left something behind.

320
00:23:17,205 --> 00:23:20,311
The ranger's famous flat
hat traces its roots to

321
00:23:20,351 --> 00:23:23,170
the wide-brimmed campaign
hats of the Army.

322
00:23:28,940 --> 00:23:31,650
So the Army saved Yellowstone from people.

323
00:23:31,690 --> 00:23:34,024
But there's one more
part of this story that

324
00:23:34,064 --> 00:23:36,330
the early park leaders liked to leave out.

325
00:23:38,110 --> 00:23:41,034
Superintendent Norris
claimed that Native people

326
00:23:41,074 --> 00:23:43,690
were afraid of the
geysers and stayed away.

327
00:23:43,730 --> 00:23:47,440
The Park Service says the
evidence shows the opposite.

328
00:23:47,480 --> 00:23:50,820
Tribes had known this land
for thousands of years.

329
00:23:52,470 --> 00:23:58,690
And in the summer of 1877, just five years
after the park was created, as many as 800

330
00:23:58,730 --> 00:24:04,730
Nez Perce men, women and children crossed
Yellowstone, running from the U.S. Army.

331
00:24:05,790 --> 00:24:09,155
Dawn, August 24, 1877.

332
00:24:09,195 --> 00:24:12,362
In the Lower Geyser Basin,
a group of tourists

333
00:24:12,402 --> 00:24:15,430
from Montana is camped
near Fountain Geyser.

334
00:24:15,470 --> 00:24:19,290
They've been in the park ten
days, enjoying the sights.

335
00:24:20,930 --> 00:24:23,585
That morning, riders
appear out of the steam.

336
00:24:23,625 --> 00:24:25,885
They are Nez Perce warriors.

337
00:24:25,925 --> 00:24:30,142
And behind them, crossing the
world's first national park,

338
00:24:30,182 --> 00:24:34,180
are as many as 800 men, women
and children, with nearly

339
00:24:34,220 --> 00:24:37,890
2,000 horses, running from
the United States Army.

340
00:24:39,730 --> 00:24:44,163
Before the day is over, one of those
tourists, George Cowan, will be shot

341
00:24:44,203 --> 00:24:48,145
in the leg and then in the head,
and left for dead in the forest.

342
00:24:48,185 --> 00:24:49,675
He will survive.

343
00:24:49,715 --> 00:24:53,040
He will crawl about 12
miles over four days.

344
00:24:54,680 --> 00:24:59,180
This is the story of the 13 days
when a war crossed Yellowstone.

345
00:24:59,220 --> 00:25:02,218
The Park Service says
it may be the only war

346
00:25:02,258 --> 00:25:05,325
ever fought inside an
existing national park.

347
00:25:05,365 --> 00:25:08,789
And of the people the Army
was chasing, who were not

348
00:25:08,829 --> 00:25:12,760
invaders here, but families
trying to save their own lives.

349
00:25:22,930 --> 00:25:26,285
The Nez Perce call themselves
the nimíipuu, "the people."

350
00:25:26,325 --> 00:25:31,450
Their homeland stretched across what
is now Oregon, Idaho and Washington.

351
00:25:33,230 --> 00:25:37,554
In 1863, the United States
rewrote an earlier treaty

352
00:25:37,594 --> 00:25:41,415
and cut their reservation
by about 90 percent.

353
00:25:41,455 --> 00:25:43,935
Many Nez Perce never agreed to it.

354
00:25:43,975 --> 00:25:46,090
They still call it the Thief Treaty.

355
00:25:47,880 --> 00:25:51,385
One band was led by a chief
the Americans called Joseph.

356
00:25:51,425 --> 00:25:54,195
His own name was Hinmatóowyalahtq̓it.

357
00:25:54,235 --> 00:25:57,686
His band refused to give
up its home in Oregon's

358
00:25:57,726 --> 00:26:01,540
Wallowa Valley, as his father
had refused before him.

359
00:26:01,580 --> 00:26:07,885
In the spring of 1877, the Army ordered
them onto the smaller reservation.

360
00:26:07,925 --> 00:26:11,990
Before they could get there, violence
broke out, and war followed.

361
00:26:13,810 --> 00:26:18,460
So five bands of Nez Perce did the
only thing they could. They ran.

362
00:26:18,500 --> 00:26:22,081
Over the mountains into
Montana, hoping to find safety

363
00:26:22,121 --> 00:26:25,340
with the Crow, and later
hoping to reach Canada.

364
00:26:27,110 --> 00:26:29,987
On August 9, at a place
called the Big Hole,

365
00:26:30,027 --> 00:26:32,440
soldiers attacked their camp at dawn.

366
00:26:32,480 --> 00:26:36,170
Many Nez Perce were killed,
including women and children.

367
00:26:36,210 --> 00:26:39,350
The survivors kept moving south and east.

368
00:26:39,390 --> 00:26:41,220
Straight toward Yellowstone.

369
00:26:47,050 --> 00:26:50,530
This is Henrys Lake, in
Idaho, just west of the park.

370
00:26:50,570 --> 00:26:55,178
On August 20, some 30 miles west
of here, Nez Perce warriors

371
00:26:55,218 --> 00:26:59,980
raided the Army's camp at night
and ran off most of its mules.

372
00:27:00,020 --> 00:27:03,528
The general in charge,
Oliver Otis Howard, stopped

373
00:27:03,568 --> 00:27:06,620
here for days to rest
and resupply his men.

374
00:27:08,410 --> 00:27:12,145
Within days, by about August
23, the Nez Perce entered

375
00:27:12,185 --> 00:27:15,750
Yellowstone, near where the
West Entrance is today.

376
00:27:17,570 --> 00:27:22,295
Leading them through the park was a man
named Poker Joe, also called Lean Elk.

377
00:27:22,335 --> 00:27:26,285
He was of mixed descent, spoke
English, and knew the country.

378
00:27:26,325 --> 00:27:29,219
Leadership was shared
among several chiefs,

379
00:27:29,259 --> 00:27:32,495
including Looking Glass,
a respected war leader.

380
00:27:32,535 --> 00:27:35,642
Joseph's role was mostly
to care for the camp:

381
00:27:35,682 --> 00:27:38,350
the elders, the women and the children.

382
00:27:40,170 --> 00:27:43,304
They followed the Madison
and Firehole rivers

383
00:27:43,344 --> 00:27:45,985
upstream, into the Lower Geyser Basin.

384
00:27:46,025 --> 00:27:48,630
And that's where they
ran into the tourists.

385
00:27:54,220 --> 00:27:58,975
The tourists were a party of nine,
most of them from Radersburg, Montana.

386
00:27:59,015 --> 00:28:02,747
Among them were George Cowan,
his young wife Emma, and

387
00:28:02,787 --> 00:28:06,310
Emma's brother and sister,
Frank and Ida Carpenter.

388
00:28:06,350 --> 00:28:08,320
They had come to see the geysers.

389
00:28:09,940 --> 00:28:14,685
The warriors took their horses and
supplies and forced them to come along.

390
00:28:14,725 --> 00:28:17,525
The party's wagon was
abandoned in the forest

391
00:28:17,565 --> 00:28:20,090
along what's now called Nez Perce Creek.

392
00:28:21,900 --> 00:28:24,660
At first, Poker Joe tried to protect them.

393
00:28:24,700 --> 00:28:28,075
The tourists thought he
was Chief Joseph himself.

394
00:28:28,115 --> 00:28:32,152
He offered to let them go in exchange
for their horses and guns, and

395
00:28:32,192 --> 00:28:35,790
he warned them that some of the
younger warriors were angry.

396
00:28:37,580 --> 00:28:40,755
At the base of Mary Mountain,
those warnings came true.

397
00:28:40,795 --> 00:28:44,425
George Cowan was shot through
the thigh, then in the head.

398
00:28:44,465 --> 00:28:45,830
He was left for dead.

399
00:28:47,660 --> 00:28:51,215
He wasn't dead. When he woke
up, he started to crawl.

400
00:28:51,255 --> 00:28:57,142
For four days, wounded, he dragged himself
about 12 miles through the forest, until

401
00:28:57,182 --> 00:29:02,610
scouts riding ahead of General Howard's
army finally found him on August 29.

402
00:29:04,440 --> 00:29:07,990
Meanwhile, the Nez Perce held
a council about the captives.

403
00:29:08,030 --> 00:29:14,430
On August 25, near Mud Volcano, they
let Emma, Ida and Frank go free.

404
00:29:14,470 --> 00:29:17,193
Poker Joe himself rode
with them back across

405
00:29:17,233 --> 00:29:19,870
the river, and pointed them toward safety.

406
00:29:21,650 --> 00:29:24,945
Years later, Emma Cowan
wrote about that summer.

407
00:29:24,985 --> 00:29:26,860
She remembered the terror.

408
00:29:26,900 --> 00:29:30,930
But she also remembered moments of
kindness from the people who held her.

409
00:29:36,720 --> 00:29:40,612
Earlier that same day, the whole
Nez Perce caravan had crossed

410
00:29:40,652 --> 00:29:44,290
the Yellowstone River at a
shallow place near Mud Volcano.

411
00:29:44,330 --> 00:29:47,040
Today, it's called Nez Perce Ford.

412
00:29:48,800 --> 00:29:49,800
Picture it.

413
00:29:49,840 --> 00:29:54,793
Hundreds of people, elders and children,
nearly 2,000 horses, all crossing

414
00:29:54,833 --> 00:29:59,300
the river late in the day, with
the Army somewhere behind them.

415
00:30:01,130 --> 00:30:03,870
Not every encounter went
the way of the Cowans'.

416
00:30:03,910 --> 00:30:08,066
On August 26, near Otter Creek,
close to the Grand Canyon of the

417
00:30:08,106 --> 00:30:12,590
Yellowstone, warriors attacked another
group of tourists from Helena.

418
00:30:12,630 --> 00:30:16,295
A man named Charles Kenck was killed.

419
00:30:16,335 --> 00:30:18,780
Another, Jack Stewart,
was wounded and robbed.

420
00:30:18,820 --> 00:30:24,400
A third, Ben Stone, survived by hiding
in the creek for about three hours.

421
00:30:26,040 --> 00:30:30,429
Five days later, at Mammoth, a
warrior killed another member of

422
00:30:30,469 --> 00:30:34,365
that group, Richard Dietrich,
in the doorway of a cabin.

423
00:30:34,405 --> 00:30:40,040
The man who fired had lost his own family
at the Big Hole, just three weeks earlier.

424
00:30:41,820 --> 00:30:44,648
That same day, warriors
raided a ranch a few

425
00:30:44,688 --> 00:30:47,320
miles below Mammoth and burned the house.

426
00:30:47,360 --> 00:30:50,450
Cavalry and Crow scouts
arrived soon after.

427
00:30:52,180 --> 00:30:57,040
In all, the Nez Perce met about 25
visitors in Yellowstone. Two were killed.

428
00:31:02,920 --> 00:31:05,736
General Howard followed them into the park

429
00:31:05,776 --> 00:31:08,660
with about 600 men, but
he never caught up.

430
00:31:08,700 --> 00:31:11,670
He never fought them
anywhere inside Yellowstone.

431
00:31:13,490 --> 00:31:16,403
The Nez Perce moved east
through Pelican Valley

432
00:31:16,443 --> 00:31:19,105
and into the high country
of the northeast.

433
00:31:19,145 --> 00:31:22,608
Another Army column, under
Colonel Samuel Sturgis,

434
00:31:22,648 --> 00:31:26,080
waited to trap them at the
edge of the mountains.

435
00:31:27,740 --> 00:31:31,270
Instead, the Nez Perce made a
feint, drawing Sturgis away.

436
00:31:31,310 --> 00:31:35,804
Then they slipped out through a narrow
gorge to the Clarks Fork River, through

437
00:31:35,844 --> 00:31:39,640
a gorge said to be barely wide
enough for two horses side by side.

438
00:31:39,680 --> 00:31:43,581
By the time Sturgis realized
what had happened and rejoined

439
00:31:43,621 --> 00:31:47,655
Howard, the soldiers were two
days and about 50 miles behind.

440
00:31:47,695 --> 00:31:51,480
The Nez Perce had spent
13 days in Yellowstone.

441
00:31:51,520 --> 00:31:52,910
And they were still free.

442
00:31:58,730 --> 00:32:01,455
They kept going north for
more than three weeks.

443
00:32:01,495 --> 00:32:06,000
By the end, they had
traveled about 1,170 miles.

444
00:32:07,630 --> 00:32:10,067
At the end of September, in the Bears Paw

445
00:32:10,107 --> 00:32:12,665
Mountains of Montana,
they stopped to rest.

446
00:32:12,705 --> 00:32:15,485
They were about 40 miles from Canada.

447
00:32:15,525 --> 00:32:19,380
Another Army force, under
Colonel Nelson Miles, attacked.

448
00:32:21,250 --> 00:32:24,455
The siege lasted five
days, in freezing cold.

449
00:32:24,495 --> 00:32:26,460
Poker Joe was killed.

450
00:32:26,500 --> 00:32:28,965
So was Joseph's brother, Ollokot.

451
00:32:29,005 --> 00:32:33,250
So was Looking Glass, shot when he
stepped up onto a bluff, out of cover.

452
00:32:35,000 --> 00:32:40,150
On October 5, 1877, Joseph
handed over his rifle.

453
00:32:40,190 --> 00:32:43,205
More than 400 Nez Perce surrendered.

454
00:32:43,245 --> 00:32:48,016
That night, another chief, White Bird,
slipped away with a small group,

455
00:32:48,056 --> 00:32:52,190
and a few hundred Nez Perce in
all eventually reached Canada.

456
00:32:53,920 --> 00:32:57,695
You may know the words Joseph is
said to have spoken that day:

457
00:32:57,735 --> 00:33:01,550
"From where the sun now stands,
I will fight no more forever."

458
00:33:03,290 --> 00:33:05,150
The truth is more complicated.

459
00:33:05,190 --> 00:33:09,262
Those words were written down,
and rewritten many times,

460
00:33:09,302 --> 00:33:13,080
by an Army officer named
Charles Erskine Scott Wood.

461
00:33:13,120 --> 00:33:16,650
One historian counted
19 published versions.

462
00:33:16,690 --> 00:33:19,435
But people who were
there agreed that Joseph

463
00:33:19,475 --> 00:33:22,260
said something very
close to that last line.

464
00:33:28,120 --> 00:33:30,475
The survivors were not allowed to go home.

465
00:33:30,515 --> 00:33:35,960
432 of them were sent first to Fort
Leavenworth, in Kansas, then to Indian

466
00:33:36,000 --> 00:33:41,815
Territory, in what is now Oklahoma, where
many of the newborns and elders died.

467
00:33:41,855 --> 00:33:46,219
Only in 1885 were they allowed
back to the Northwest, and Joseph

468
00:33:46,259 --> 00:33:49,935
was never allowed to live in
his Wallowa Valley again.

469
00:33:49,975 --> 00:33:54,505
He died in 1904 on the Colville
Reservation in Washington.

470
00:33:54,545 --> 00:33:59,790
The Park Service describes him as dying
brokenhearted, an exile from his home.

471
00:34:01,620 --> 00:34:06,822
Today, the route of the Nez Perce is a
National Historic Trail, and the Nez

472
00:34:06,862 --> 00:34:11,505
Perce National Historical Park
preserves places across four states.

473
00:34:11,545 --> 00:34:14,829
Many Nez Perce consider
the trail sacred, and they

474
00:34:14,869 --> 00:34:18,260
still come to Yellowstone
to honor their ancestors.

475
00:34:19,900 --> 00:34:24,696
Rebecca Miles, the first woman to chair
the Nez Perce Tribe, put it simply,

476
00:34:24,736 --> 00:34:29,250
looking back on that history: "There's
no future without forgiveness."

477
00:34:30,940 --> 00:34:35,428
So the next time you drive past
the Firehole River, or cross the

478
00:34:35,468 --> 00:34:40,310
Yellowstone near Mud Volcano, remember:
this wasn't empty wilderness.

479
00:34:40,350 --> 00:34:43,870
People were here, and some of them
were running for their lives.

